 "k-means is a special case that assumes equal, round clusters and gives hard labels. A Gaussian mixture models the data as a blend of Gaussians, each with its own mean, spread and weight, so clusters can be elliptical and different in size, and each point gets a probability of membership, which is useful for borderline customers or for scoring. It is fitted with expectation-maximization, alternating between computing memberships and updating the Gaussians, and can get stuck in local optima. More components always fit better, so I'd choose the number with BIC or held-out likelihood. k-means is faster and simpler when clusters are roughly round and hard labels are enough.",
