Q("algo-019","What does PCA do, when would you use it, and what do you need to do to the data before running it?","ML Algorithms","medium",C,"Mid-level DS interview",
 [("How do you decide how many components to keep?","test explained variance and scree plots"),
  ("Can you interpret the components?","test loadings and their limits"),
  ("When is PCA a bad idea?","test non-linear structure, tree models and interpretability needs")],
 "PCA rotates the data onto the directions of greatest variance, so it depends on feature scale and compresses linear structure only.",
 "It reduces dimensions by keeping the components with the most variance.",
 "PCA finds orthogonal directions that capture the most variance and projects the data onto the first few. That compresses correlated features, removes noise, and helps visualization or models hurt by collinearity. Because it chases variance, features must be standardized or large-scale features dominate. I'd choose components from cumulative explained variance or a scree plot, or by downstream model performance. Components are linear mixtures, so interpretation relies on loadings and gets hard. It is a poor fit when structure is non-linear, when tree models don't need it, or when you must explain individual features to stakeholders.",
 ["dimensionality-reduction","feature-scaling"],["pca","feature-scaling"],"concept"),
Q("algo-020","A colleague shows a t-SNE plot with clear clusters and says it proves the data has natural groups. How do you respond?","ML Algorithms","hard",C,"Senior DS interview, unsupervised learning",
 [("What do distances between clusters in a t-SNE plot mean?","test that global distances are not meaningful"),
  ("How do perplexity and other settings change the picture?","test sensitivity to parameters"),
  ("How would you check that the clusters are real?","test clustering in the original space and stability")],
 "t-SNE and UMAP preserve local neighborhoods, not global distances or cluster sizes, so a picture of clusters is a hypothesis, not evidence.",
 "t-SNE is only for visualization, so I'd be careful.",
 "I'd say the plot is a good prompt but not proof. t-SNE (and UMAP to a lesser degree) preserves local neighborhoods while distorting global structure, so cluster sizes, the gaps between clusters and their relative positions aren't reliable. The picture also changes with perplexity, initialization and random seed, and can show apparent clusters in structureless noise. To test it I'd re-run with several settings and seeds, cluster in the original or PCA space and compare, check stability under resampling, and see whether the groups differ on outcomes the embedding never saw.",
 ["dimensionality-reduction","clustering","edge-cases"],["t-sne","clustering"],"concept"),
Q("algo-021","You have 2,000 candidate features and 10,000 rows. How do you select features?","ML Algorithms","medium",C,"Mid-level DS interview",
 [("What is the difference between filter, wrapper and embedded methods?","test the taxonomy"),
  ("How can feature selection cause leakage?","test selecting on the full data before cross-validation"),
  ("When would you not bother selecting?","test regularized and boosted models")],
 "Feature selection has to happen inside each cross-validation fold. Otherwise the selection step itself leaks information from the validation data.",
 "Remove low-variance and correlated features, then use importance from a model.",
 "With many more features than the data supports, I'd start with cheap filters: drop constants, near-duplicates and highly correlated pairs, and apply domain judgment. Embedded methods such as lasso or tree-based importance then rank what remains, and wrapper methods such as recursive elimination can refine it at higher cost. The key discipline is doing selection inside each cross-validation fold; selecting on the whole dataset first leaks the labels into the validation score and inflates it. With regularized models or boosting, aggressive selection is often unnecessary, but it can help stability, speed and explainability.",
 ["feature-selection","data-leakage","validation-strategy"],["feature-selection","cross-validation"],"concept"),
Q("algo-022","When does stacking or blending models help, and when is it not worth it?","ML Algorithms","hard",C,"Senior DS interview, applied ML",
 [("Why must the second-level model be trained on out-of-fold predictions?","test leakage in stacking"),
  ("How different do the base models need to be?","test correlation of errors"),
  ("What does it cost in production?","test maintenance and latency")],
 "Ensembling helps when the base models make different mistakes. When they are highly correlated or the system must stay simple, the gain doesn't justify the complexity.",
 "Combining models usually gives better accuracy, as in Kaggle.",
 "Averaging or stacking helps when models make different, partly uncorrelated errors, such as a boosted tree with a linear model or a neural net. Stacking trains a second-level model on the base models' out-of-fold predictions; using in-sample predictions would leak and overstate the benefit. Blending is the simpler version using a holdout set. Gains are typically small on clean tabular data where several boosted models are highly correlated. The costs are real: more training time, latency, monitoring and failure modes. I'd try a simple average first and ship the ensemble only if the gain beats the cost of maintaining it.",
 ["ensembling","model-selection","tradeoffs"],["stacking"],"concept"),
Q("algo-023","Only 2% of your cases are positive. Compare resampling (including SMOTE), class weights and threshold tuning.","ML Algorithms","medium",C,"Mid-level DS interview, imbalanced data",
 [("Why can SMOTE hurt if applied before splitting the data?","test leakage through synthetic samples"),
  ("How does class imbalance change which metric you use?","test precision-recall metrics over accuracy"),
  ("Which approach would you try first, and why?","test preferring simple options")],
 "Imbalance is mostly a metric and threshold problem. Resampling changes what the model sees, so it must happen only inside training folds and often adds little over class weights.",
 "Use SMOTE to balance the classes.",
 "First I'd fix evaluation: accuracy is meaningless at 2%, so I'd use precision-recall, PR AUC and cost-based thresholds. Then compare options on honest folds. Class weights are the simplest: they change the loss without altering the data. Random undersampling is cheap but discards information; oversampling duplicates rows; SMOTE interpolates new minority points and can create unrealistic samples and overlap, and it must be applied only inside the training fold or synthetic points leak into validation. Resampling also distorts predicted probabilities, so I'd recalibrate. I'd try weights plus threshold tuning first and keep resampling only if it measurably helps.",
 ["class-imbalance","evaluation-metrics","data-leakage"],["smote"],"case"),
Q("algo-024","How would you tune hyperparameters, and how do you avoid overfitting to your validation set while doing it?","ML Algorithms","medium",C,"Mid-level DS interview",
 [("Why is random search often better than grid search?","test that only a few hyperparameters matter"),
  ("What does Bayesian optimization do differently?","test sequential, model-based search"),
  ("What is nested cross-validation for?","test an unbiased estimate after tuning")],
 "Every look at the validation score while tuning leaks a little information, so the final estimate needs data the tuning never touched.",
 "Use grid search with cross-validation.",
 "I'd first narrow the space to what matters for the model; for boosting that is learning rate, depth, number of rounds (with early stopping) and regularization. Random search usually beats a grid because only a few hyperparameters really matter and random draws try more distinct values of each; Bayesian optimization (for example Optuna) learns from earlier trials to choose promising ones and is worth it when each run is expensive. Repeated tuning on the same folds overfits them, so I'd keep a final holdout or use nested cross-validation to estimate the tuned procedure's performance, and stop when gains are within noise.",
 ["hyperparameter-tuning","validation-strategy"],["hyperparameter","cross-validation"],"concept"),
